# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
euthydemus-learn.md:5: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r3 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.2 have nothing from it; euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.3 has "learns(cleinias)"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
euthydemus-learn.md:5: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "cleinias is a learner, one who learns what the teacher teaches" is false. Is S3, "And they are the teachers of those who learn--the grammar-master and the lyre-master used to teach you and other boys; and you were the learners?", true then?
euthydemus-learn.md:9: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r3 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.2 have "∀x (learns(x) → ¬knows(x))"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.3 has "¬knows(cleinias)"; they differ when only "cleinias already knows the things cleinias is learning" is true, which the first allows
euthydemus-learn.md:9: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "cleinias already knows the things cleinias is learning" is true and "cleinias is a learner, one who learns what the teacher teaches" is false. Is S5, "And when you were learners you did not as yet know the things which you were learning?", true then?
euthydemus-learn.md:13: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r3 has "∀x (learns(x) → ¬wise_k(x))"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "cleinias is a learner, one who learns what the teacher teaches" and "cleinias is wise, in the sense of already knowing what is being learned (S7)" are true, which the second allows
euthydemus-learn.md:13: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r3, to: Suppose "cleinias is a learner, one who learns what the teacher teaches" and "cleinias is wise, in the sense of already knowing what is being learned (S7)" are true. Is S7, "And were you wise then?", true then?
euthydemus-learn.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r3 has "∀x (wise_c(x) → ¬unlearned(x))"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.2 has "∀x (wise_k(x) → knows(x))"; they differ when only "cleinias already knows the things cleinias is learning", "cleinias is wise, in the sense of already knowing what is being learned (S7)" and "cleinias is unlearned" are true, which the first allows; euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.3 has "∀x (wise_k(x) → knows(x))" and "∀x (wise_c(x) → ¬unlearned(x))"
euthydemus-learn.md:31: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 3 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r3 has "∀x (learns(x) → wise_c(x) ∧ ¬unlearned(x))"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.2 has "∀x (learns(x) → wise_k(x) ∧ ¬unlearned(x))"; they differ when only "cleinias is a learner, one who learns what the teacher teaches", "cleinias is wise, in the sense of already knowing what is being learned (S7)" and "cleinias is one of the wise boys, in the sense of the clever, apt pupils (S15-S16)" are true, which the first allows; euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.3 has "∀x (learns(x) → wise_c(x) ∧ ¬unlearned(x)) ∧ ¬∀x (learns(x) → unlearned(x) ∧ ¬wise_k(x))"
euthydemus-learn.md:31: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered yes, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r3 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "cleinias is a learner, one who learns what the teacher teaches", "cleinias is wise, in the sense of already knowing what is being learned (S7)" and "cleinias is one of the wise boys, in the sense of the clever, apt pupils (S15-S16)" are true and "cleinias is unlearned" is false. Is the conclusion, "Then after all the wise are the learners and not the unlearned; and your last answer to Euthydemus was wrong.", true then?
euthydemus-learn.logic.r3: argue/readings: U1: euthydemus-learn.logic.r3 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.3 take for granted "for every x, if x is one of the wise boys, in the sense of the clever, apt pupils (S15-S16), then it is not so that x is unlearned"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.2 does not
euthydemus-learn.logic.r3: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r3 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r3.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "for every x, if x is one of the wise boys, in the sense of the clever, apt pupils (S15-S16), then it is not so that x is unlearned"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
